[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"NgRx in Angular: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"NgRx is a powerful library for managing state in Angular applications, inspired by Redux. It provides a predictable and centralized way to handle application state, making it easier to reason about and debug complex applications. This tutorial will guide you through the core concepts of NgRx: Actions, Reducers, Selectors, and Effects."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Actions: Defining Events"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Actions are plain JavaScript objects that represent events happening in your application. They are the *only* way to trigger a state change. Each action has a 'type' property, which is a string that uniquely identifies the action. Actions can also carry a 'payload' containing data related to the event."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// src/app/store/actions/product.actions.ts
import { createAction, props } from '@ngrx/store';
import { Product } from '../models/product.model';

export const loadProducts = createAction('[Product] Load Products');
export const loadProductsSuccess = createAction('[Product] Load Products Success', props<{ products: Product[] }>());
export const loadProductsFailure = createAction('[Product] Load Products Failure', props<{ error: string }>());

export const addProduct = createAction('[Product] Add Product', props<{ product: Product }>());"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we define four actions using 'createAction' from '@ngrx/store'. 'loadProducts' is a simple action with no payload. 'loadProductsSuccess' and 'loadProductsFailure' carry payloads with the products data and error message, respectively. 'addProduct' carries the new product to be added."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Reducers: Handling State Changes"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reducers are pure functions that take the current state and an action as input and return the new state. They are responsible for updating the application state based on the actions dispatched.  It's crucial that reducers are pure functions, meaning they should not have side effects and should always return the same output for the same input."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// src/app/store/reducers/product.reducer.ts
import { createReducer, on } from '@ngrx/store';
import { Product } from '../models/product.model';
import * as ProductActions from '../actions/product.actions';

export interface ProductState {
  products: Product[];
  loading: boolean;
  error: string | null;
}

export const initialState: ProductState = {
  products: [],
  loading: false,
  error: null,
};

export const productReducer = createReducer(
  initialState,
  on(ProductActions.loadProducts, (state) => ({ ...state, loading: true })), 
  on(ProductActions.loadProductsSuccess, (state, { products }) => ({ ...state, products, loading: false, error: null })), 
  on(ProductActions.loadProductsFailure, (state, { error }) => ({ ...state, loading: false, error })), 
  on(ProductActions.addProduct, (state, { product }) => ({ ...state, products: [...state.products, product] }))
);
"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, 'createReducer' is used to define the reducer function. The 'on' function associates actions with state updates. For example, when 'loadProducts' is dispatched, the 'loading' flag is set to 'true'. When 'loadProductsSuccess' is dispatched, the 'products' array is updated with the data from the payload, and 'loading' is set to 'false'.  The spread operator ('...') is used to create a new state object without mutating the original state."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Selectors: Querying the State"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selectors are functions that derive pieces of state from the store. They are used to efficiently select, derive, and compose pieces of state. Selectors are memoized, meaning they only recalculate when their input state changes, improving performance."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// src/app/store/selectors/product.selectors.ts
import { createFeatureSelector, createSelector } from '@ngrx/store';
import { ProductState } from '../reducers/product.reducer';

export const selectProductState = createFeatureSelector<ProductState>('products');

export const selectAllProducts = createSelector(
  selectProductState,
  (state: ProductState) => state.products
);

export const selectProductsLoading = createSelector(
  selectProductState,
  (state: ProductState) => state.loading
);

export const selectProductsError = createSelector(
  selectProductState,
  (state: ProductState) => state.error
);
"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"'createFeatureSelector' is used to select a specific feature slice of the state (in this case, 'products'). 'createSelector' is then used to create selectors that derive specific pieces of data from the feature state. 'selectAllProducts' selects the 'products' array, 'selectProductsLoading' selects the 'loading' flag, and 'selectProductsError' selects the 'error' message."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Effects: Handling Side Effects"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effects are used to handle side effects, such as making HTTP requests, interacting with local storage, or performing asynchronous operations. They listen for specific actions and then perform the side effect. After the side effect is completed, they dispatch new actions to update the state."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// src/app/store/effects/product.effects.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { Actions, createEffect, ofType } from '@ngrx/effects';
import { ProductService } from '../../services/product.service';
import * as ProductActions from '../actions/product.actions';
import { catchError, map, mergeMap, of } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable()
export class ProductEffects {

  loadProducts$ = createEffect(() =>
    this.actions$.pipe(
      ofType(ProductActions.loadProducts),
      mergeMap(() =>
        this.productService.getProducts().pipe(
          map(products => ProductActions.loadProductsSuccess({ products })), 
          catchError(error => of(ProductActions.loadProductsFailure({ error: error.message })))
        )
      )
    )
  );

  constructor(private actions$: Actions, private productService: ProductService) {}
}
"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'createEffect' is used to define an effect that listens for the 'loadProducts' action. When this action is dispatched, the effect calls the 'getProducts' method of the 'ProductService'. If the request is successful, it dispatches the 'loadProductsSuccess' action with the products data. If the request fails, it dispatches the 'loadProductsFailure' action with the error message. 'ofType' filters the stream of actions to only process the 'loadProducts' action. 'mergeMap' allows us to flatten the observable returned by 'this.productService.getProducts()'."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Setting up NgRx in your Angular application"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Install NgRx packages: 'npm install @ngrx/store @ngrx/effects @ngrx/store-devtools --save'"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Import 'StoreModule.forRoot(reducers)' in your AppModule, where 'reducers' is an object mapping feature names to reducer functions."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Import 'EffectsModule.forRoot([ProductEffects])' in your AppModule to register your effects."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Import 'StoreDevtoolsModule.instrument({ maxAge: 25, logOnly: environment.production })' in your AppModule to enable the Redux DevTools for debugging (only in development mode)."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"// src/app/app.module.ts
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { StoreModule } from '@ngrx/store';
import { EffectsModule } from '@ngrx/effects';
import { StoreDevtoolsModule } from '@ngrx/store-devtools';
import { environment } from '../environments/environment';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { productReducer } from './store/reducers/product.reducer';
import { ProductEffects } from './store/effects/product.effects';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule,
    StoreModule.forRoot({ products: productReducer }),
    EffectsModule.forRoot([ProductEffects]),
    StoreDevtoolsModule.instrument({ maxAge: 25, logOnly: environment.production })
  ],
  providers: [],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }
"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"6. Dispatching Actions and Selecting State in Components"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"// src/app/components/product-list/product-list.component.ts
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { Store, select } from '@ngrx/store';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { Product } from '../../models/product.model';
import * as ProductActions from '../../store/actions/product.actions';
import * as ProductSelectors from '../../store/selectors/product.selectors';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-product-list',
  templateUrl: './product-list.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./product-list.component.css']
})
export class ProductListComponent implements OnInit {
  products$: Observable<Product[]>;
  loading$: Observable<boolean>;
  error$: Observable<string | null>;

  constructor(private store: Store) { }

  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.store.dispatch(ProductActions.loadProducts());
    this.products$ = this.store.pipe(select(ProductSelectors.selectAllProducts));
    this.loading$ = this.store.pipe(select(ProductSelectors.selectProductsLoading));
    this.error$ = this.store.pipe(select(ProductSelectors.selectProductsError));
  }

  addProduct(): void {
    const newProduct: Product = { id: 4, name: 'New Product', price: 99.99 };
    this.store.dispatch(ProductActions.addProduct({ product: newProduct }));
  }
}
"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the component, we inject the 'Store' service. In 'ngOnInit', we dispatch the 'loadProducts' action to trigger the loading of products. We then use the 'select' method to subscribe to the 'selectAllProducts', 'selectProductsLoading', and 'selectProductsError' selectors, which provide the data to display in the component's template. The 'addProduct' function dispatches the 'addProduct' action with a new product."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provides a basic overview of NgRx. By understanding Actions, Reducers, Selectors, and Effects, you can effectively manage state in your Angular applications and build more maintainable and scalable code."}]
